From: fedriani at ebd.csic.es (=?utf-8?q?Jose_Mar_=C3=AD_a_Fedriani_Laffitte?=)
Date: Thu, 03 Apr 2003 20:00:39 -0800
Subject: Question on generalized linear models
Message-ID: <BAB24367.1EFB%fedriani@ebd.csic.es>

Dear list,

    I have fitted two generalized linear models, one of them for
presence/absence data and the other one for percentage-data.  Given the
binomial nature of both sets of data, I have first assumed binomial errors
and used logit link-functions.  Although both models did converge, the one
based on percentage-data showed evidenced of a poor goodness-of-fit to the
data.  In other words, the residual deviance was to large to assumed that
the model did fit the data.  In order to improve the goodness-of-fit of my
model, I tried using other link functions but it did not work.  Then, I
tried using Poisson error (and log link function) and got a model that
fitted pretty well the data (i.e. the residual deviance was very small).
Furthermore, when I fitted a model with Poisson error using the
presence/absence data, the goodness-of-fit was much better that when using
the binomial error.

MY QUESTION:  Is correct to assume Poisson error for presence/absence and
percentage data in order to improve the goodness-of-fit of a generalized
linear models?

    Very grateful for your assistance, Jose

