1.  An array is a vector with a dim attributed.  We can change the dim
attribute at will, e.g.:

(A <- array(1:8, dim=c(4,2)))
     [,1] [,2]
[1,]    1    5
[2,]    2    6
[3,]    3    7
[4,]    4    8
> dim(A) <- c(2,2,2)
> A
, , 1

     [,1] [,2]
[1,]    1    3
[2,]    2    4

, , 2

     [,1] [,2]
[1,]    5    7
[2,]    6    8

2.  "?t" reveals that the array generalization of transpose is "aperm",
e.g.:

> aperm(A, 3:1)
, , 1

     [,1] [,2]
[1,]    1    3
[2,]    5    7

, , 2

     [,1] [,2]
[1,]    2    4
[2,]    6    8

Is this what you want?

One can also make an array of lists, e.g.:

> B <- array(list(1:2, 3:4, 5:6, 7:8), dim=c(2,2))
> B[1,2]
[[1]]
[1] 5 6

